  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nEarly morning river valley fog development is expected once\nagain beneath strong high pressure, cool overnight lows, and \nlight to calm wind. Akin to previous nights, don't think that it\nleaks out of the valleys very much. Fog will dissipate after \nsunrise as temperatures begin to rise.\n\nThe expansive surface high dominates through the rest of the \nweek and the first half of this coming weekend with a bone dry \ncolumn. This will allow for diurnal mixing to add a degree or \ntwo to afternoon highs and erode a few degrees off of dew \npoints. The minimum RH Wednesday afternoon at PIT dropped to \n22%, so it will be quite dry. With the surface high meandering \neast and promoting modest southerly gradient flow today, we \nshould now be starting the first of likely three days with \nafternoon highs in the 60s for the vast majority of the area. 70\ndegrees isn't even out of the question on Saturday south of \nPittsburgh where 30-50% probabilities sneak in.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nOur next chance for rain is still on track to arrive late this \nweekend as a trough arrives from the west. Previously mentioned \nuncertainty has begun to clear itself up a bit as we're starting to \nsee a bit better agreement with the two features we've been \nmonitoring the past couple days as they unfold upstream. It does \nappear that we're favoring a less phased solution with a faster \nshortwave sliding southwest through Manitoba, and the southern \nstream energy ejecting out of the Rockies losing its steam with\neastward progression. This points toward overall lower rain \ntotals, and we're seeing that trend in the NBM as the 90th \npercentile wetter end of the goal posts is decreasing and now is\nonly as high as 0.2-0.3\". Areawide chance for >0.1\" peaks \naround 35% along the PA and WV ridgeline and is 15-25% \nelsewhere.",
